-- ALAO script-side profiler (idea I-048). Measurement only - this never ships
-- with a rewrite, it is its own overlay mod that goes on TOP of both arms.
--
-- Why: fps measures the whole frame and ALAO only ever moves the script slice of
-- it, so every per-frame delta we queued came back null inside the +-2% noise.
-- The engine hands Lua a profile_timer (lua_help.script:1200, start/stop/time),
-- so we can time the script slice directly instead.
--
-- What it hooks: axr_main.make_callback. _g.SendScriptCallback funnels every
-- scripted callback in the game through that one function, so wrapping it gets
-- actor_on_update / npc_on_update / monster_on_update / on_key_press / ... in
-- one place, with no edit to any winner file. Optionally (off by default, see
-- WRAP_BINDERS) the three binder :update methods too.
--
-- Which copy of axr_main is live does not matter to us, and that is the point
-- of patching the table instead of editing a file: priority in this install is
-- highest-priority enabled mod, then GAMMA's loose in-place patches in
-- Anomaly/gamedata/scripts, then the .db archives, and for axr_main.script the
-- loose GAMMA copy wins - its make_callback dispatches through
-- spairs(intercepts[name], sort_func_values_ascend), i.e. the hspairs min-heap
-- from _g_patches.script, not the db copy's bare pairs. Nothing in the install
-- caches axr_main.make_callback into a local, so one assignment catches every
-- caller. Binder wrapping is the part that DOES care: bind_monster.script is
-- loose-patched by GAMMA while bind_stalker.script comes from the db.
--
-- Attribution is INCLUSIVE and top-level only: a make_callback that happens
-- while another one is already being timed is counted (as `nested`) but not
-- timed, because one shared timer cannot be nested. So the per-name numbers add
-- up to the total without double counting, and that total is the thing we are
-- actually after: script ms per frame.
--
-- Output: one block of ALAOPROF| lines in the engine log every DUMP_EVERY_MS.
-- lab/framework/aalo/profiler.py parses it back. Units are established at
-- startup against os.clock, not assumed - see calibrate().

local DUMP_EVERY_MS   = 30000   -- dump window length, engine ms
local CALIB_MS        = 250     -- busy-loop length used to establish timer units
local OVERHEAD_ITERS  = 20000   -- iterations used to price our own instrumentation
local WRAP_BINDERS    = false   -- see the header comment: binder wrapping swallows
                                -- the callbacks fired inside the binder bodies
local WRAP_LISTENERS  = false   -- v2, see "per-listener attribution" below

-- --- hitch attribution (I-058) ----------------------------------------------
-- The mean hides what the player feels. ActorMenu_on_before_init_mode is ~10 ms
-- per inventory open; at 215 fps that is two whole frames gone. So on top of
-- sum/count, record for every callback and every listener: the biggest single
-- call, when the first slow call happened, and a coarse log2 histogram so a p99
-- can be read off without keeping a sample list.
--
-- The hot path must not get more expensive - drx_da_main alone is 353 listener
-- calls per frame - so ALL of this is behind one numeric compare: a call that
-- costs less than HITCH_FLOOR_MS pays exactly `if d >= hfloor then` and nothing
-- else, no table lookup, no allocation. Above the floor we do one hash lookup,
-- a doubling loop of at most HITCH_BUCKETS steps, and one array increment; the
-- bucket array is allocated once per name, the first time that name goes slow.
--
-- Consequence, and it is deliberate: the hitch numbers only describe calls at
-- or above the floor. Everything below is "fast", counted by `calls` on the cb
-- line, and the parser reconstructs bucket 0 as calls - sum(buckets). "First
-- call" therefore means first call ABOVE THE FLOOR, which for an inventory open
-- (every one of them is milliseconds) is the first open of the session.
local HITCH             = true
local HITCH_FLOOR_MS    = 0.1   -- bucket 1 is [floor, 2*floor)
local HITCH_BUCKETS     = 14    -- 0.1 ms .. 819 ms, last bucket is open-ended

-- --- per-call trace for a named listener (I-063) -----------------------------
-- max / first / histogram is the right summary for "is this listener a hitch",
-- and the wrong one for "WHY is this one call 19 ms and that one 4". For
-- ui_inventory's open the answer is suspected to be state that grows between
-- opens (the cell pool, the grid), and a summary cannot show a correlation.
--
-- So: name a listener (or a prefix of its label) here and every one of its
-- calls gets one `ALAOPROF|1|trace|` line with its duration, the frame, and a
-- pair of cheap covariates sampled immediately BEFORE and AFTER the call - both
-- outside the timer, so the trace never inflates the number it is reporting.
--
-- This is deliberately NOT general. It is for listeners that fire a handful of
-- times a session. Point it at actor_on_update and it will write a line per
-- frame until it hits TRACE_MAX_LINES and stops.
--
-- nil (the default) means the whole thing costs one upvalue read and one `if`
-- per wrapped call - the same class of cost as the hitch floor compare, and the
-- default overlays keep that cost whether or not they use the feature.
local TRACE_LISTENERS   = nil   -- e.g. {"ActorMenu_on_before_init_mode#ui_inventory.script"}
local TRACE_MAX_LINES   = 400   -- hard cap, so a bad pattern cannot flood the log

-- =========================================================================
-- I-062 WALKOUT ADDITIONS start here. Everything below this banner is new;
-- everything above it is the I-048 + I-058 build, byte for byte, so a merge
-- with another agent's profiler change only ever conflicts inside a block
-- that is marked I-062.
--
-- The question: walking out of the gammabaseline start area produces 1-4
-- frames of 25-44 ms, CPU bound, and the hitch profiler shows NO listener
-- above ~12 ms in them. make_callback is one door into Lua and the engine has
-- several others, so the instrument is looking through a keyhole. This build
-- opens the other doors and, more importantly, adds a FRAME-level line that
-- says how much of a slow frame was script at all.
--
-- Three new wrap families, each on its own timer and its own depth guard:
--
--   bnd  object binders and se_* server objects, per CLASS and METHOD
--        (`bind_stalker.actor_binder.net_spawn`). The existing WRAP_BINDERS
--        mode is left switched off and untouched: it shares `depth` and the
--        main timer with make_callback, so a wrapped binder :update makes
--        every callback inside it `nested` and the I-048 ranking collapses.
--        That is why it was never usable, and the fix is a separate axis,
--        not a separate flag.
--   eng  functions the engine calls by name, never through make_callback:
--        visual_memory_manager.get_visible_value, ProcessEventQueue,
--        xr_logic.issue_event, ...
--   evt  CreateTimeEvent / AddUniqueCall / level.add_call bodies, wrapped at
--        REGISTRATION so each one is labelled with its registration site
--        (file:line from debug.getinfo).
--
-- Double counting is handled by ONE shared counter, `active`: a timed region
-- entered while nothing else is timed is "top level", and only top-level
-- durations go into the per-frame union. So bnd time that contains cb time is
-- counted once, on the outer axis, while both axes still get their own
-- attribution. That is the whole trick, and it is why the per-callback
-- ranking of this build is still comparable with every I-048 number.
--
-- Return values: the wrappers propagate at most THREE. net_spawn returns a
-- boolean and the engine believes it, so a wrapper that dropped returns would
-- break the game rather than mismeasure it. Nothing on the wrap lists returns
-- more than one value; do not add a variadic-return function to them.
--
-- Errors: a wrapper does not pcall. If the wrapped body throws, the timer
-- stays started and the axis wedges - same as the I-048 build, deliberately,
-- because pcall changes error semantics in a game that relies on them.
-- =========================================================================

-- Bumped whenever the instrument changes in a way that makes two runs
-- non-comparable. v2 = the first in-game run's two faults fixed: luabind class
-- objects are userdata so nothing was wrapped, and deferred functors were
-- labelled by _g.script's plumbing instead of their owner.
-- v3 = the second in-game run's findings:
--   * the deferred functor the log blamed for a 26 ms frame at t~54 s was MY
--     OWN eng wrapper around ProcessEventQueue, wrapped a second time by the
--     evt hook. Never double-wrap our own wrappers.
--   * the `@caller` half of a functor label pointed at this file, because the
--     debug.getinfo level was counted from inside two helper frames and a
--     pcall. Capture the caller in the outer wrapper, where the depth is known.
--   * a new `sub` axis and `nst` lines, to get inside
--     bind_smart_terrain.smart_terrain_binder.update, which is 440-457 ms in
--     ONE call in all four captures with only 6 ms of callbacks in it.
-- v4 = what the hamlet run (20260920-201819-I-062-a83793) answered and asked:
--   * the 440 ms IS se_smart_terrain:try_respawn, 99.97% of the call, in all
--     four captures. update_jobs never appears and smr_pop.smart_can_respawn
--     (the gate) is 3 ms across 750 calls, so the cost is entirely AFTER the
--     gate: one squad being created on the frame the actor arrives.
--   * so v4 follows the spawn path down, and every level of it gets its OWN
--     axis, because two functions on one axis mean the inner one is `nested`
--     and invisible - which is how a 440 ms call had nothing in it to look at
--     for two runs. bnd -> sub -> spn -> mem -> acr is five independent
--     timers for five levels of one call stack.
--   * per-axis nst floors, so a 27 ms evt body gets a breakdown too.
local WALKOUT_VER       = 4

local WRAP_BINDERS_I062 = true  -- the bnd axis (see above); independent of the
                                -- old WRAP_BINDERS, which stays off
local WRAP_BINDER_UPDATE = true -- :update is the only per-object PER-FRAME
                                -- method. Off leaves the rare-but-expensive
                                -- lifecycle methods wrapped for ~free.
local WRAP_GLOBALS      = true  -- the eng axis
local WRAP_TIME_EVENTS  = true  -- the evt axis
local RESCAN_EVERY      = 600   -- frames between re-attempts at wrap targets
                                -- that were not loaded yet. A module can be
                                -- pulled in lazily by the first _G reference
                                -- to it, so "not there at on_game_start" is
                                -- not "not there".
local RESCAN_ROUNDS     = 20    -- give up re-scanning after this many

-- the frame recorder
local FRAMES            = true
local FRM_FLOOR_MS      = 12    -- emit a line only for frames at least this long
local FRM_MAX_LINES     = 400   -- hard cap: a bad scene must not flood the log

-- --- v3: getting inside one very slow call --------------------------------
-- Run 2 found `bind_smart_terrain.smart_terrain_binder.update` at 440-457 ms
-- in ONE call, in all four captures, with u_cb of 6 ms for the whole frame -
-- so it is not a callback, not a listener, and the frame recorder's top-3 just
-- says "that binder". Two additions to get further:
--
--   * a `sub` axis for the se_smart_terrain methods that update() calls. It is
--     its own axis for the same reason bnd is: a sub region nested inside the
--     bnd region has to be timed, not counted as `nested`. The LEAF functions
--     go on the existing `eng` axis instead of on `sub`, so one level of
--     nesting inside sub is timed too, without a fifth axis.
--   * an `nst` line: when a TOP-LEVEL region takes at least NST_FLOOR_MS, dump
--     the five most expensive regions that completed inside it, whatever axis
--     they were on. That is the actual breakdown of one bad call.
local NST               = true
local NST_FLOOR_MS      = 50
-- v4: per axis, because what counts as "worth a breakdown" is not the same
-- everywhere. The t~54 s frame is a 27 ms time-event body (it turned out to be
-- DynamicNewsManager.TickNews) and sat under a 50 ms floor for two runs.
local NST_FLOOR_BY_AXIS = { cb = 20, evt = 20, lst = 20 }
local NST_MAX_LINES     = 120
local NST_SLOTS         = 5

-- What to wrap. Generated from lab/tools/i062_engine_entry_census.py over the
-- live winner tree (1350 scripts, 38 object_binder classes, 36 cse_/se_ server
-- classes), ranked by how likely a thing is to run when objects switch online.
-- Each entry is {module (the .script name), class, ...}; a class that is not
-- there at install time is reported as missing in the hdr line and re-tried.
local BINDER_TARGETS = {
	-- NPCs and monsters first: these are what goes online when you walk out
	{ "xr_motivator",          "motivator_binder"        },
	{ "bind_monster",          "generic_object_binder"   },
	{ "bind_stalker",          "actor_binder"            },
